## RQ1: How do students report studying?

Students were asked to describe how they usually study for their classes in an open-ended fashion (Table 2). The results suggest a large percentage of students reported switching among topics or projects in blocks, suggesting intentional pacing or studying (46.55%). Illustrating this point, one student wrote the following, “I usually study in increments…”. Relatedly, many also indicated taking breaks (40%). Smaller, but still substantial numbers of students acknowledged reviewing course material (28.45%), reviewing their notes (27.59%), and a smaller number of students noted creating or finding an environment amenable to studying (for them; 14.66%). Other habits were documented by a smaller percentage of students (Table 2).

## RQ2: What activities do students engage in during breaks?

Students were asked to select from a list which activities they engaged in during their breaks. Figure 1 presents a bar graph of activities in descending order. Note that students could select more than one activity. The most common activities include eating (*n* = 98), checking social media (*n* = 95), listening to music (*n* = 64), and watching TV (*n* = 61), reported by half or more of the sample.

